id: regexp/case-insensitive/m2-kelvin-and-long-s/0001
area: regexp
op: RegExp.prototype.test
in: str:"[a-z]" str:"i" str:"\u212A"
out: value: bool:false
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row M2 (as widened 2026-11-01, owner's decision D-widen): Kelvin U+212A against [a-z] or k without u, by the i flag or by (?i:), and the Ohm sign U+2126 against [U+03C9] or [U+03A9] under i without u; Broiler.JS answers true: .NET's casing under RegexOptions.IgnoreCase (BuiltIns/RegExp/JSRegExp.cs, ParseFlags), where Canonicalize's toUppercase leaves each sign itself; a correction of Broiler.JS (JSD-0081); answered by SJL-11.5, Broiler.JS's cut-over to Broiler.Regex, when the translator retires
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexp.prototype.test spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexpbuiltinexec spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilepattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilesubpattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilecharacterclass spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compiletocharset spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-runtime-semantics-canonicalize-ch ref:regexpref@1 ucd:17.0.0/UnicodeData.txt ucd:17.0.0/SpecialCasing.txt
script: new RegExp("[a-z]", "i").test("\u212a")
note: correspondence row M2: Canonicalize without u is toUppercase, which leaves U+212A and maps U+017F to S, kept only within ASCII; under u and v it is simple case folding
since: 31

