id: regexp/case-insensitive/m2a-dotted-and-dotless-i/0001
area: regexp
op: RegExp.prototype.test
in: str:"i" str:"i" str:"\u0130"
out: value: bool:false
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row M2a: the dotted and dotless i, i and I against U+0130 and U+0131, in literals, classes and back-references, without u; Broiler.JS answers, under tr-TR, true for i and U+0130 and for I and U+0131, and under lt-LT true for i and I against U+0130, with the negated classes false: RegexOptions.IgnoreCase without CultureInvariant reads .NET's current culture; under the invariant culture it answers as Canonicalize does, and a run there reports the field as no longer holding; a correction of Broiler.JS (JSD-0081; decision D33, its bundle sjl-11-001's part D); answered by SJL-11.5, Broiler.JS's cut-over to Broiler.Regex
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexp.prototype.test spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexpbuiltinexec spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilepattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilesubpattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-runtime-semantics-canonicalize-ch ref:regexpref@1 ucd:17.0.0/UnicodeData.txt ucd:17.0.0/SpecialCasing.txt
script: new RegExp("i", "i").test("\u0130")
note: correspondence row M2a: Canonicalize reads no culture: the Turkic and Lithuanian mappings are not its, so every case holds in every host culture (Broiler.JS's part D)
since: 31

